{'answer': "Yemen played a pivotal role in the early cultivation and spread of coffee, being regarded as the origin of the beverage. It was here that Coffea arabica was cultivated as a crop on a large scale rather than just being gathered from the wild. Yemen became the first major production zone and global exporter of coffee, dominating the trade for two centuries. By the fifteenth century, coffee cultivation had established itself in Yemen's highland regions, where it was harvested, roasted, and brewed by Sufi circles during their nightly prayers.

Yemen developed a thriving coffee economy centered in its western highlands by the late sixteenth century, cultivating coffee on terraced slopes. The port of Mocha was particularly important, serving as a significant trade connection with Jeddah, Cairo, and beyond. During the seventeenth century, the demand for coffee from Yemen grew so rapidly that it rivaled many commodities of the global spice trade, and until the end of that century, Yemen was the world’s principal coffee producer and exporter.

Modern genetic studies have confirmed Yemen's foundational role in the global spread of Coffee arabica, showing that a vast majority of cultivated Arabica varieties trace back to plants domesticated in Yemen.", 'model_used': 'gpt-4o-mini', 'prompt_tokens': 4572, 'completion_tokens': 242, 'total_tokens': 4814}
